<!--
  EXPERIMENTAL. Hart CPS joint class: at every grid point, computes the
  lower thermal wind (HVTL), the upper thermal wind (HVTU) and parameter
  B (the right-minus-left difference of layer thickness between the two
  500 km semicircles either side of the steering flow, see cps_HB.xml)
  all at once, and buckets the triple (B, HVTL, HVTU) into a single
  7-code class (cps_HartCPS.hart_class) using Hart's own strict lines -
  B against 10 m, both thermal wind terms against 0 m, no neutral band:

    0  symmetric deep warm core     B <= 10, lower VT >= 0, upper VT >= 0
    1  symmetric shallow warm core  B <= 10, lower VT >= 0, upper VT <  0
    2  asymmetric deep warm core    B >  10, lower VT >= 0, upper VT >= 0
    3  asymmetric shallow warm core B >  10, lower VT >= 0, upper VT <  0
    4  asymmetric cold core         B >  10, lower VT <  0
    5  symmetric cold core          B <= 10, lower VT <  0
    6  shallow cold core (lower cold, upper warm)  any B, lower VT < 0, upper VT >= 0

  (row 6 takes precedence over rows 4/5 whenever lower VT < 0 and upper
  VT >= 0 at once - see cps_HartCPS.hart_class's own docstring for the
  full ordering rationale). This one field carries all three parameters
  (B, HVTL, HVTU) at once - see cps_HartCPS.py's module docstring,
  "Parameter B and the joint class" and "Relation to Hart's storm-centered
  diagrams" sections, for why a single joint class is preferred over
  reading two separate categorical fields side by side. NaN outside a
  closed low in mean sea level pressure (cps_HartCPS.closed_low_mask, the
  same MSLP mask HCPSidx uses - see cps_HCPSidx.xml's comment for why
  MSLP), below ground (a NaN HVTL), or wherever B is NaN (the
  steering-flow speed below MIN_STEERING_MS - rare, but see
  cps_HartCPS.parameter_b_grid's docstring).

  TWELVE METHOD BLOCKS, tried in order; AWIPS runs the first whose inputs
  the model carries. Two independent choices are folded into one ordered
  list, because the framework has no way to say "any of these MSLP
  spellings" separately from "whichever band set the model carries":
  the band set is the outer loop (so the level choice always wins over
  the MSLP spelling), the MSLP input the inner one.
    Methods 1-4   cps_HartCPS.executeHartClass7: Hart's own levels - the
                  lower term over 900-600 hPa and the upper over 600-300
                  hPa at 50 hPa spacing (thirteen GH Fields, 600 hPa once),
                  B on the 900-600 hPa thickness with layerScale 1.0. Runs
                  on a model that carries every 50 hPa level from 900 to
                  300 hPa (the GFS).
    Methods 5-8   cps_HartCPS.executeHartClassMean2: the standard levels -
                  the lower term is the mean of the 925/850/700 and
                  850/700/500 hPa slopes, the upper term 500/400/300 hPa,
                  B on the 925-700 hPa thickness rescaled by layerScale
                  1.4548 to a 900-600 hPa equivalent. Runs on every other
                  model that carries the six standard levels.
    Methods 9-12  cps_HartCPS.executeHartClass: the 925/850/700 lower band
                  this package shipped with, same inputs as methods 5-8.
                  Because its inputs are identical to the two-slope mean's,
                  AWIPS never reaches it on its own: it is the documented
                  fallback for a site that deletes methods 5-8 (or 1-8) to
                  reproduce earlier numbers.
  Within each group of four the MSLP input is, in order, MSLP at MSL
  (the baseline derived parameter behind the Volume Browser's "MSL
  Press", which aliases each model's own field), PMSL at MSL (verified
  on GFS at OPC), MSLP at Surface, then GH at 1000MB with the trailing
  pmslKind constant 1 (height converted to MSLP at 8 m per hPa, for a
  model with no MSLP field at all). ECMWF did not load with PMSL alone
  and does with this ordering.

  Hemisphere: same as cps_HB.xml - the coriolis pseudo-field's sign is the
  hemisphere factor parameter B uses; a 2D field gets the correct sign
  on both sides of a grid straddling the equator.

  Below-ground masking: every height Field below (the band levels) is
  blanked (NaN) before its own computation wherever it is below ground
  for its own pressure, using the surface pressure field (P, Surface)
  and capHpa - see cps_HartCPS.mask_below_ground and cps_HCPSidx.xml's
  comment for the same convention. capHpa (default 900 hPa) is a cap,
  not the literal level pressure: it keeps a deep low's own legitimately
  low surface pressure over open water from being mistaken for terrain,
  while still masking the Greenland ice sheet and Iceland highlands at
  925/900/850 hPa. The MSLP Field is not masked (it is defined
  everywhere); the class is blanked over terrain because HVTL is.

  VERIFY: same checks as cps_HB.xml and cps_HCPSidx.xml (GH abbreviation,
  fixed-level Field spelling including the 50 hPa levels in the GFS
  inventory, P abbreviation and its Surface level spelling, the
  MSLP/PMSL abbreviations and level spellings, the coriolis pseudo-field's
  abbreviation, dx/dy pseudo-fields and ConstantField support, levels=
  value, unit="" acceptance), and that your build accepts a Method with
  33 arguments (methods 1-4; the previous revision's 26 loaded). If this
  definition fails to load, replace the coriolis Field with
  <ConstantField value="1.0"/> at the same position in every block to
  assume the Northern Hemisphere.
-->
